#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "bb7ae99a-b8d9-5e4d-906c-9e18d22c1404" :question/math-academy-id "e-8742" :question/problem "Calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf{F}-3\\mathbf{G})$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\left(x^2+y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ \\left(xy-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\quad \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{G}=x+y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-3G) & =∇\\cdot (F)+∇\\cdot (-3G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-3∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+x-2y \\\\  & =3x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-3G) & =∇\\cdot F-3∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =(3x-2y)-3\\cdot (x+y) \\\\  & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7de054d4-8384-5aab-b2c4-a43b1f86a16c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8928" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 1x, xy \\right \\rangle$ and $f(x,y) = \\dfrac 1{x},$ calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ and $\\nabla f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x)+⟨\\frac{1}{x},xy⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08050a00-d7c5-50f1-adb8-385171303ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566381} {:answer/id #uuid "13f66efb-6f99-576a-8818-22852dc76ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566401} {:answer/id #uuid "48f0b34d-93c2-5f6e-a27c-884c035464a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566505} {:answer/id #uuid "d2420004-c2e0-5e7f-a0f9-afa99a76bcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566776} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f43fbb-2eda-595f-9545-880fc4dbfa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f0b34d-93c2-5f6e-a27c-884c035464a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566505} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fefd2ee-4647-59e3-ba09-c04f194c181b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55481d4d-7e9b-585b-a201-3605d4c46b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105632" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (F-G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{1}{y}\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot (-G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d2669f-748a-5d3e-add4-044dc27f9818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489595} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2395f7-fab5-51f4-8be4-159b63c3d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489773} {:answer/id #uuid "c917523d-92c4-54de-b0df-a6bf816b85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489953} {:answer/id #uuid "d31bc03e-05c6-5840-8321-3067c96b1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489983} {:answer/id #uuid "e786bbf5-86e3-5cad-83cf-c31ac0c9a27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c917523d-92c4-54de-b0df-a6bf816b85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04136be-f727-5add-9a25-13584d652f7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe02f258-a8c1-593e-bacb-46d349ae065b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105634" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (-3F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-4z\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4z) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2) \\\\  & =6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67a4c6d5-5df8-56f1-9562-1183cdf52efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566559} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1883ee-aa7b-58a8-bb69-91db9d890b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6y-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566699} {:answer/id #uuid "d939463c-2773-55d3-a9df-82e0ba8ed130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566800} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d39e62-edf8-59d7-939f-9ffd94a4ab70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566860} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ea0ebf-190f-5b1b-a961-7ed7a9e394dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9ea0ebf-190f-5b1b-a961-7ed7a9e394dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566865} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e54fc4-1cd5-5a2a-baab-1f0122eeb649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e36ebfc7-87b5-5b58-93d4-2765b6334842" :question/math-academy-id "q-105636" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (F-5G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+xy\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-5G) & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot (5G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-5∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =2x+x \\\\  & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-5G) & =∇\\cdot F-5∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =3x-5\\cdot (x-y) \\\\  & =3x-5x+5y \\\\  & =5y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04afe455-673f-5b0e-a6fd-b296db94bc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505114} {:answer/id #uuid "4665da7c-47c6-56a6-8a63-2cd951c60e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505249} {:answer/id #uuid "54b3d62c-789b-5344-8eb8-1b87f779cb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505274} {:answer/id #uuid "da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505523} {:answer/id #uuid "e85c3dd7-de42-550d-b590-57c43fa3e773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-12y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffd1f69-02c8-5ee4-adf9-edd55635cbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c768058-b351-51d4-a894-a96f2a63f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105639" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (3F+2G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (3F+2G) & =∇\\cdot (3F)+∇\\cdot (2G) \\\\  & =3∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (3F+2G) & =3∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))+2\\cdot (3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})+6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482bad03-d104-5acf-908e-4de36f9d24ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566504} {:answer/id #uuid "9b596be5-4a27-5a25-8996-e2d3bbbbd944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566666} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ac01f6-3dc1-5a87-a4eb-b1aff42f923a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566795} {:answer/id #uuid "db7a205d-c26e-58ab-8f31-fa9f77756582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566804} {:answer/id #uuid "ded4ca36-9107-50e5-be1b-8bc4a9c4f529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7ac01f6-3dc1-5a87-a4eb-b1aff42f923a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566795} :answer-field/id #uuid "009133ed-6611-51da-ad44-898a727e4ec5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d754555e-e33d-5cf1-883c-d6ab614cfeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105650" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩$ and $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩ \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡y-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡y \\\\  & =({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)+({cos}^{2}⁡y-{sin}^{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8e7f9a-6d64-5d22-b5bc-95c71fed9a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(x+y+2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489613} {:answer/id #uuid "48f71578-9dc8-5af1-8c23-0a20717bf08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+2y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489684} {:answer/id #uuid "e167ec05-7ba8-54ee-88eb-33b8c7129f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490006} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dde9b5-7512-5c76-9578-51e87312e497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(x+2y+3z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490057} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a0413-baf6-5112-9242-7dcd5ce3749b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(2x+y+2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e167ec05-7ba8-54ee-88eb-33b8c7129f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490006} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f30e86d-bd3c-561f-bb1d-7773101df310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19772385-86fe-59c3-9b8a-1626c506e897" :question/math-academy-id "q-105656" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨xy,zy,zx⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(zy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(zx)=y+z+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+⟨xy,zy,zx⟩\\cdot ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}yz \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+xyz(x+y+z) \\\\  & =2xyz(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f94f982-0ecc-59a6-adaf-f7ec5b3b3e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+4{z}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566642} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b6ddb9-22f4-5665-999c-a2365d2e697e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566695} {:answer/id #uuid "c14122a1-1a79-54c5-aea2-b82491bcefab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566745} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7a3bff-3924-5992-8a52-1650dee8c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+4yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566769} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e5191c-50f6-512c-8fea-8605b2ae8810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd7a3bff-3924-5992-8a52-1650dee8c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+4yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566769} :answer-field/id #uuid "663a5ef1-2806-5743-91be-acd7d7ce6565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3654dbb4-b86f-5d3a-b4a5-c4e336a8010d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105657" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨y\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,z\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2},$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,4y,6z⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0+0+0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)\\cdot 0+⟨y\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,z\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩\\cdot ⟨2x,4y,6z⟩ \\\\  & =2xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+4yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-12{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505192} {:answer/id #uuid "40bcbcfd-b0a9-5f9e-b620-2401616f5850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-12{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505229} {:answer/id #uuid "51f59a6c-efd5-5c01-9c0b-9478a549ed93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xyz+{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505267} {:answer/id #uuid "558eb743-ed39-5b77-8c92-39d8417ede55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505275} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c811ea-cec4-5a24-807f-3cb9271e236c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16xyz-12{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-12{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505192} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2ee70c-3ea3-5fe5-8d90-cd968896e62f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c942870-19a7-5088-91ca-fc84364e3087" :question/math-academy-id "q-131243" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (-4F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF={x}^{2}yz\\,i+({y}^{3}-y)\\,j-xy{z}^{2}\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-4F)=-4∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xyz+3{y}^{2}-1-2xyz \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-4F) & =-4∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (3{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-12{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39889727-a276-5682-8ef7-8911020878d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(3xz+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505204} {:answer/id #uuid "4441e299-9c2b-5890-9b2f-cf910acd93da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(1+xz+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505243} {:answer/id #uuid "5670544a-efa4-5dea-bb60-d1317fd8ae11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(1+3yxz+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505277} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f47d0b-4c4f-560a-896d-281726884e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(1+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505452} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(1+3xz+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xz(1+3xz+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505481} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba8f018-adaf-5841-94d1-797bd241ddf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37458a2a-1dd8-5f4b-97d7-084609fd0a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-131291" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x{z}^{3},{z}^{2}⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2xz,0,{x}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})=\\frac{1}{x}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z(\\frac{1}{x}+2z)+⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x{z}^{3},{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,0,{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =xz+2{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =xz+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =xz(1+3xz+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbac9b0-3131-5b68-8747-b8662452e7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489587} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2f2f0d-40d7-5f2d-83f1-2ea1f486329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489614} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9781e6-d514-50c9-8bc5-ef65fd44af25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489650} {:answer/id #uuid "727ac25f-a90a-5dcd-bcbf-04adfe3d45f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489781} {:answer/id #uuid "cc634756-be7d-5a9e-b487-b504317b94d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b9781e6-d514-50c9-8bc5-ef65fd44af25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c37c60e-e97b-5fd7-8819-529b45993a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bc87287-f16b-573d-aff7-5fb6dedac503" :question/math-academy-id "q-98888" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩$ and $f(x,y)=xy,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)=1+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xy(1+{x}^{2})+⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩\\cdot ⟨y,x⟩ \\\\  & =xy+{x}^{3}y+xy+{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy+2{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy(1+{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
